  <!-- ============ S3_WHY_CARB ============ -->
  <section id="why-carb">
    <div class="wrap measure">
      <h2>Here's Why We Put a Carbohydrate in a Hydration Product</h2>
      <p>Your small intestine absorbs glucose and sodium together.</p>
      <p>That's one of the basic principles behind oral rehydration. When carbohydrate is available alongside sodium, it participates in the transport process your body uses to absorb sodium and fluid.</p>
      <p>The difference with palatinose is how slowly it becomes available.</p>
      <p>Regular sugar is broken down quickly. Palatinose breaks down more slowly and is absorbed along the full length of the small intestine.</p>

      <figure class="mechanism-img">
        <img src="{{ 'mechanism-sticks.webp' | asset_url }}" width="900" height="805" alt="Valor Vitals B1 Blue Raspberry stick packs, the single-serving format that mixes into water" loading="lazy">
      </figure>

      <p>So instead of having the carbohydrate available for a short period, it remains available for that sodium-glucose transport process over a longer window.</p>
      <p>If you've ever heard of someone taking casein protein before bed because it releases amino acids more slowly than other proteins, it's a similar idea.</p>
      <p>Slow release is the point.</p>
      <p>We wanted a hydration product for long days, so using a carbohydrate that gets burned through quickly didn't make much sense to us.</p>
      <p>And using no carbohydrate at all meant leaving out part of the absorption equation.</p>
      <p>So we added palatinose.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

